.TH BE-AZERTY-SETTINGS 1 "2026-10-30" "belgian-azerty-windows 0.9.9" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
be-azerty-settings \- window to switch the Belgian keyboard profile and numpad decimal key
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B be-azerty-settings
.SH DESCRIPTION
Opens the "Belgian keyboard" window (also in the app menu). It offers the four
combinations of the profile (Windows: Ctrl+Alt works as AltGr and Caps Lock as
on Windows, except on GNOME (Zorin), where Caps Lock stays Linux unless
.B be-azerty-fix
is run with CAPS=windows; Linux standard: Caps Lock for letters only) and the numpad decimal
key (period or comma), with the current one selected. After a choice it asks
for an administrator password and runs
.B be-azerty-fix system PROFILE=... DECIMAL=...
through
.BR pkexec (1).
The button "Fast typing" opens a tick box for the optional ibus skip of the
Terminal, the text editor and VS Code, off unless ticked (see
.BR be-azerty-fix (8),
.BR ibus-skip ).
It is per user and asks for no password; it runs
.BR "be-azerty-fix ibus-skip auto on" | off .
The texts follow the language of the session (Dutch, French, German, otherwise
English). Needs
.BR zenity (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR be-azerty-fix (8)
